# OWNER / MIGRATION connection. Owns the schema (every table, every
# sequence, the audit_log_append_only() trigger function) and is the only
# credential `pnpm --filter @idm/api db:migrate` ever connects with — never
# used by the running app or the sync worker. See RUNTIME_DATABASE_URL below
# and docs/archive/audits/audit-integrity.md finding H1 / docs/02-architecture.md's
# "The two database roles" section for why the split exists.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://idm:idm_dev_password@localhost:5432/identity_manager
# RUNTIME connection. What the API process and the SyncWorker actually
# connect as: full SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on ordinary tables, but only
# SELECT/INSERT on audit_log — no UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE, no ownership, no
# CREATE on the schema, so this role cannot ALTER/DROP/CREATE OR REPLACE any
# object, including the append-only trigger function itself.
# `db:migrate` (run as the OWNER above) provisions this exact role from this
# URL's own username/password — see apps/api/src/db/roles.ts. Required: the
# app fails to boot rather than falling back to DATABASE_URL if this is unset.
RUNTIME_DATABASE_URL=postgres://idm_app:idm_app_dev_password@localhost:5432/identity_manager
KEYCLOAK_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080/realms/identity-manager
KEYCLOAK_AUDIENCE=idm-api
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=idm-sync-service
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET=idm_sync_dev_secret_change_me
# (Organizations, Task 8) A service account in Keycloak's `master` realm
# holding the `create-realm` role. Required ONLY to create organizations;
# everything else in this system works without it, and leaving both lines
# COMMENTED OUT is a supported configuration — POST /organizations then answers
# 503 NOT_CONFIGURED instead of writing a row that could never provision.
#
# Commented rather than present-but-empty, deliberately. `FOO=` does not mean
# "unset": Node's --env-file (which every script in apps/api/package.json uses)
# sets the value to the empty string, and loadEnv rejects an empty credential on
# purpose (apps/api/test/env.spec.ts) — a blank secret is an accident, and
# failing at startup beats a client-credentials grant with an empty secret and a
# confusing 401 from Keycloak. Shipping these two blank made `db:migrate` refuse
# to start on a clean clone.
#
# A SEPARATE credential from KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET above, and
# necessarily so: that one lives in the `identity-manager` realm and holds
# realm-management roles there, while POST /admin/realms is a server-level
# endpoint only a master-realm account can call.
#
# To create it: re-run the setup script with the opt-in flag —
#   SETUP_PROVISIONER=1 bash scripts/keycloak-setup.sh
# and copy the two values it prints at the end. By hand: in the master realm,
# add a client, turn OFF standard flow, turn ON "Service accounts roles", then
# under Service account roles assign `create-realm` and nothing else.
#KEYCLOAK_PROVISION_CLIENT_ID=
#KEYCLOAK_PROVISION_CLIENT_SECRET=
PORT=3000
# Starts the outbox-draining SyncWorker alongside `start:dev`/production
# boot. Defaults to "true" when absent; set to "false" to run the app
# without it (e.g. a secondary instance behind a load balancer).
SYNC_WORKER_ENABLED=true
# Ceiling on physical Postgres connections the API's pool will ever open.
# Defaults to 10 (pg's own default) when absent.
DB_POOL_MAX=10
# Milestone 14, Task 9 (connector console) — a resolvable credential for the
# in-repo echo connector target, so the console's own dry-run/apply flow (and
# its Playwright E2E) has a real environment variable to name via
# connector_targets.config.credentialSecretName. Not read by anything unless
# a connector_targets row is configured to name it — see connectors/secrets.ts.
CONNECTOR_ECHO_CREDENTIAL=dev_echo_secret_change_me

# Sub-project 4 (mail server connector) — the service token for the mail
# server's provisioning API. Issued THERE via POST /api/v1/idm/tokens
# (superadmin JWT), returned exactly once, and stored on that side only as a
# SHA-256 hash. Referenced BY NAME from connector_targets.config's
# `tokenSecretName`, never stored in this database — see connectors/secrets.ts.
#
# Nothing reads this unless a connector_targets row for 'mail_server' names
# it. Setup runbook: docs/11-operations.md ("Mail server over a separate host").
CONNECTOR_MAIL_SERVER_TOKEN=

# The idm-sso-admin client secret, printed by scripts/keycloak-setup.sh.
#
# A DIFFERENT credential from KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET above: that one is
# idm-sync-service, which holds exactly four realm-management roles and cannot
# touch OIDC clients. This one holds manage-clients and nothing else, and is
# resolved ONLY by the sso_app code path -- so the user and group sync path
# does not merely decline to mint clients, it does not hold the capability.
#
# Nothing reads this unless a connector_targets row for 'keycloak_sso' names
# it as credentialSecretName. Only needed if you register SSO applications.
CONNECTOR_KEYCLOAK_SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Used ONLY by `pnpm --filter @idm/api smoke:mail` — the contract check that
# proves what this connector builds is what the mail server accepts.
# MAIL_SMOKE_EMAIL must be in a domain ALREADY hosted there; the mail server
# never auto-creates domains.
MAIL_SERVER_BASE_URL=
MAIL_SMOKE_EMAIL=

# --- Connector secret naming (security audit) -------------------------------
# A connector may ONLY resolve environment variables named CONNECTOR_*.
#
# connector_targets.config is admin-editable and names BOTH the variable to
# read AND the destination host, so without this rule an operator could set a
# target's credentialSecretName to DATABASE_URL, point it at a host they
# control, and receive the database password in an Authorization header.
# Namespacing makes that structurally impossible rather than merely
# discouraged — see apps/api/src/connectors/secrets.ts.
#
# Anything you add for a connector must therefore start with CONNECTOR_.
